1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,303
At twenty-seven minutes and fifty-three
seconds past one in the morning,

2
00:00:03,640 --> 00:00:05,180
a duty officer in Baltimore gave

3
00:00:05,180 --> 00:00:06,706
an order to stop the traffic.

4
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:11,280
Sixty-seven seconds later the bridge
was in the river, and six men who had been filling

5
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:12,596
potholes on it were gone.

6
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:15,180
Everyone who could act, acted.

7
00:00:15,640 --> 00:00:18,240
So how did the sixty-seven seconds run out?

8
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:21,300
There is a particular kind of American

9
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,360
job that only exists after midnight.

10
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:26,860
The road belongs to everybody
during the day, so the work

11
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:29,220
on it has to happen when nobody wants it.

12
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:30,520
Cones.

13
00:00:30,800 --> 00:00:32,116
A truck with a light bar.

14
00:00:32,640 --> 00:00:34,480
A crew that knows each other's coffee

15
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:36,160
order and works in the cold

16
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,792
with the river underneath them.

17
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:41,820
On the night of the twenty-fifth
of March, 2024, that crew

18
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:43,520
was on the Francis Scott Key Bridge

19
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:45,300
in Baltimore, patching the deck.

20
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:48,160
Eight people were up there —

21
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:50,640
a seven-person road maintenance
crew and one inspector.

22
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:53,283
Below them, at the Seagirt Marine Terminal,

23
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:55,678
a container ship was finishing her cargo.

24
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:57,303
She was called the Dali.

25
00:00:57,880 --> 00:00:59,512
She flew the flag of Singapore,

26
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:02,140
and her international number was 9697428.

27
00:01:04,660 --> 00:01:09,100
She had come to the United States on the
nineteenth of March, worked Newark for two days,

28
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:10,820
then Norfolk for one,

29
00:01:11,260 --> 00:01:12,660
and moored in Baltimore Harbor

30
00:01:12,660 --> 00:01:14,980
on the twenty-third at thirty-six
minutes past

31
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:15,980
two in the morning.

32
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:17,700
She was a working ship

33
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:18,800
on a working schedule,

34
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:20,820
and she was bound for Sri Lanka.

35
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:22,460
She was loaded.

36
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:24,180
Not riding high and empty

37
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:27,242
on her way to pick something up —

38
00:01:27,243 --> 00:01:30,952
loaded, which matters, because a loaded ship
carries her momentum differently and answers

39
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:31,885
a rudder differently.

40
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:33,768
Two halves of one picture.

41
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:36,980
The night lives in the distance
between them.

42
00:01:37,020 --> 00:01:37,870
Up on the deck,

43
00:01:37,900 --> 00:01:39,532
the work is small and physical.

44
00:01:40,180 --> 00:01:42,601
Somebody is walking backwards
guiding a truck.

45
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:45,020
Somebody is checking that a cone has

46
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:45,920
not blown over.

47
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,620
It is the kind of job

48
00:01:47,620 --> 00:01:49,500
where the most dangerous thing you expect

49
00:01:49,500 --> 00:01:52,300
is a car that does not slow down.

50
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:55,162
Down at the terminal, the work
is enormous and invisible.

51
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:59,025
A ship this size is a floating power
station with cargo strapped to it.

52
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:02,047
She makes her own electricity
from four diesel generators.

53
00:02:02,600 --> 00:02:04,758
She distributes it on a high-voltage bus.

54
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:06,980
She steps it down through transformers

55
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:09,040
to run everything a person would recognise

56
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:10,940
as a ship —

57
00:02:10,941 --> 00:02:12,820
the lights, the pumps, the steering.

58
00:02:13,180 --> 00:02:15,250
Neither group can see the other's risks.

59
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:16,600
That is not a criticism.

60
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:19,000
That is just what a port is.

61
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:21,360
And there was one more witness aboard

62
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:24,120
that neither of them
would have thought about.

63
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,720
International rules require a ship
of the Dali's tonnage and age

64
00:02:27,720 --> 00:02:30,484
to carry a voyage data recorder —

65
00:02:30,485 --> 00:02:32,537
the maritime equivalent of a black box.

66
00:02:33,100 --> 00:02:36,410
Through everything that followed,
with the ship's lighting gone twice,

67
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:38,124
the bridge audio kept recording.

68
00:02:38,180 --> 00:02:39,812
Somebody would hear this night.

69
00:02:40,440 --> 00:02:41,808
At about a quarter to one,

70
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:44,760
the senior pilot ordered the main
engine to dead slow ahead.

71
00:02:45,320 --> 00:02:47,120
That engine was not a small thing.

72
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:51,860
Fifty-five thousand six
hundred and twenty-six

73
00:02:51,861 --> 00:02:55,980
horsepower, turning one propeller. Call it
five hundred family cars. One shaft.

74
00:02:56,400 --> 00:02:57,300
One loaded ship.

75
00:02:57,620 --> 00:02:58,520
In the dark.

76
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:00,870
At about seven minutes past one,

77
00:03:00,900 --> 00:03:03,300
once she was in the channel,
the tugboats let go.

78
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:04,840
That is normal.

79
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:06,340
Every night.

80
00:03:06,840 --> 00:03:07,740
Every port.

81
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:09,740
From that moment she moved under

82
00:03:09,740 --> 00:03:10,640
her own power.

83
00:03:11,160 --> 00:03:12,107
Two pilots aboard.

84
00:03:12,680 --> 00:03:14,627
A bridge she had passed under before.

85
00:03:15,060 --> 00:03:17,060
At about twenty-five minutes past one,

86
00:03:17,600 --> 00:03:19,680
she was six tenths of a mile out.

87
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:21,236
Three of her own lengths.

88
00:03:21,540 --> 00:03:22,803
And the lights went out.

89
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:24,708
Two electrical breakers —

90
00:03:24,709 --> 00:03:26,137
HR1 and LR1 —

91
00:03:26,140 --> 00:03:27,666
opened without being told to.

92
00:03:27,960 --> 00:03:29,180
In the space of that,

93
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:31,260
the ship lost her lighting and most

94
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:32,160
of her equipment.

95
00:03:32,680 --> 00:03:34,360
She lost the pumps that cooled

96
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:35,260
the main engine.

97
00:03:35,640 --> 00:03:37,887
She lost the pumps that moved
the steering gear.

98
00:03:38,240 --> 00:03:40,220
The emergency generator started and picked

99
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:41,325
up the emergency bus.

100
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:43,428
And the crew went to work.

101
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:45,880
They closed HR1 and LR1 by hand,

102
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:47,000
and the power came back

103
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:48,053
to the whole vessel.

104
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:51,960
At twenty-six minutes and thirty-nine
seconds past one, the pilots called

105
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:52,860
for a tug.

106
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:57,736
The Eric McAllister answered immediately
and turned toward them. She was three miles away.

107
00:03:58,200 --> 00:03:59,621
She never reached the ship.

108
00:04:00,020 --> 00:04:03,020
The pilots' dispatcher got on the phone
to the Coast Guard and told them

109
00:04:02,960 --> 00:04:04,280
the Dali had lost power.

110
00:04:04,980 --> 00:04:06,280
Then, two tenths of a mile

111
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,122
from the bridge, it happened again.

112
00:04:08,760 --> 00:04:10,308
Two more breakers —

113
00:04:10,309 --> 00:04:11,785
DGR3 and DGR4 —

114
00:04:11,786 --> 00:04:13,880
opened, and this time the generators came

115
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:15,700
off the high-voltage bus entirely.

116
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:17,413
Total loss of power,

117
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:18,487
high side and low.

118
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:22,520
At twenty-seven minutes and twenty-five
seconds past one, a pilot picked

119
00:04:22,520 --> 00:04:24,960
up the radio and warned every
vessel on the water.

120
00:04:25,360 --> 00:04:26,700
Seven seconds after that,

121
00:04:26,820 --> 00:04:29,754
the duty officer in Baltimore gave
his order about the traffic.

122
00:04:30,080 --> 00:04:32,659
And thirty-one seconds
after the second blackout,

123
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:36,104
the crew closed two different breakers
by hand and got the lights back.

124
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:39,854
They got the electricity back
before the ship touched the bridge.

125
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:41,975
They did not get the propeller back.

126
00:04:42,640 --> 00:04:43,903
One more thing was true.

127
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:46,860
The emergency power could run
a single steering pump.

128
00:04:47,420 --> 00:04:49,580
But that pump, running alone on emergency

129
00:04:49,580 --> 00:04:51,900
power, was designed to turn the rudder

130
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:54,360
more slowly than it
would with all pumps working.

131
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:56,800
And a rudder needs water moving past

132
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:58,875
it to steer at all —

133
00:04:58,876 --> 00:05:02,318
so without the propeller turning, it
would have been less effective anyway.

134
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:03,665
The ship had a wheel.

135
00:05:04,420 --> 00:05:05,999
Turning it did almost nothing.

136
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:08,280
At about twenty-nine minutes past one,

137
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:10,600
the Dali struck pier number seventeen.

138
00:05:11,540 --> 00:05:12,856
The piers were not naked.

139
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:16,170
They were protected by structures
called dolphins —

140
00:05:16,200 --> 00:05:17,680
sheet pile and concrete,

141
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:19,580
built to take a hit and keep

142
00:05:19,580 --> 00:05:20,738
a ship off the column.

143
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:23,680
Two of them are drawn on the Safety
Board's own map

144
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:25,417
of the Dali's last three minutes.

145
00:05:26,100 --> 00:05:27,205
They were not enough.

146
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:29,500
Why they were not enough is still

147
00:05:29,500 --> 00:05:31,763
standing over other people's roads tonight.

148
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:33,140
The span came down.

149
00:05:33,880 --> 00:05:35,354
Eight people had been on it.

150
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:36,580
Six of them died.

151
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:40,000
Which raises the first thing
that does not fit.

152
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:41,640
If a ship losing power

153
00:05:41,640 --> 00:05:42,540
is a known event,

154
00:05:42,920 --> 00:05:44,700
and this bridge had protection built

155
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:48,380
for exactly that, why
did the protection not hold?

156
00:05:50,200 --> 00:05:51,840
Everybody gets this part wrong.

157
00:05:52,580 --> 00:05:54,140
The obvious answer is that a ship

158
00:05:54,140 --> 00:05:55,614
lost power and hit a bridge.

159
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:58,020
That is true, and it explains nothing,

160
00:05:58,020 --> 00:05:59,500
because ships lose power.

161
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,120
It is not rare.

162
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:03,840
A vessel is a small industrial city

163
00:06:03,840 --> 00:06:06,682
with four generators and several
thousand connections.

164
00:06:07,960 --> 00:06:09,710
Industrial cities have bad nights.

165
00:06:09,740 --> 00:06:11,280
So the question was never whether

166
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:12,648
the Dali could lose power.

167
00:06:13,180 --> 00:06:14,620
A ship of this class is built

168
00:06:14,620 --> 00:06:16,304
on the assumption that she will.

169
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:18,500
The whole architecture exists to make

170
00:06:18,500 --> 00:06:19,658
a blackout survivable.

171
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:22,980
Two step-down transformers, TR1 and TR2,

172
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:25,600
either of which can feed
the low-voltage side.

173
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:28,340
A breaker on each side of each transformer.

174
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:30,200
A bus tie that can be closed

175
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:31,726
to run the two halves as one,

176
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:34,020
which is how she was running that night.

177
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:35,460
And critically:

178
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:37,600
the ship's critical systems were originally

179
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:40,520
designed with reliable redundancies
and automatic

180
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:43,340
restart capabilities,
so that after a blackout

181
00:06:43,420 --> 00:06:45,330
she could get her power back quickly.

182
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:47,100
That is not an opinion.

183
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:50,580
That is what the United States
told a grand jury this year.

184
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:54,678
Now look at what the humans on the water
actually did in those four minutes.

185
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:58,664
The engineers found the breakers
and closed them by hand —

186
00:06:58,665 --> 00:07:00,920
twice, under a blackout,
at night, in a channel.

187
00:07:01,820 --> 00:07:05,523
The pilots called for a tug within seventy-four
seconds of the first blackout.

188
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:08,421
The pilots' dispatcher called
the Coast Guard.

189
00:07:08,640 --> 00:07:11,120
A pilot broadcast a warning to everything

190
00:07:11,120 --> 00:07:12,436
floating in the Patapsco.

191
00:07:13,040 --> 00:07:14,987
The duty officer stopped the traffic.

192
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:16,979
The tug turned around and ran.

193
00:07:17,700 --> 00:07:21,060
Every one of those is a person doing exactly
the thing their training says

194
00:07:21,060 --> 00:07:22,920
to do, in the right order,

195
00:07:23,200 --> 00:07:25,095
faster than most of us would manage.

196
00:07:25,520 --> 00:07:26,994
The tug was three miles out.

197
00:07:27,390 --> 00:07:28,640
The bridge was two tenths

198
00:07:28,640 --> 00:07:29,540
of a mile ahead.

199
00:07:30,160 --> 00:07:32,002
Nobody was going to close that gap.

200
00:07:32,440 --> 00:07:35,540
And the second wrong answer usually
arrives right here.

201
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:39,460
If the tug could not get there
in time, then surely the mistake

202
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:40,780
was letting the tugs go

203
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:41,780
in the first place.

204
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:43,120
It was not.

205
00:07:43,640 --> 00:07:44,798
That is the procedure.

206
00:07:45,760 --> 00:07:46,920
Tugs escort a ship out

207
00:07:46,920 --> 00:07:49,680
of the berth and through the tight
water, and once

208
00:07:49,680 --> 00:07:53,547
she is in the channel and making way
under her own power, they are released.

209
00:07:54,020 --> 00:07:55,700
About seven minutes past one,

210
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:57,740
in the channel, that is what happened,

211
00:07:58,360 --> 00:07:59,720
and the Safety Board records

212
00:07:59,720 --> 00:08:00,878
it as normal practice.

213
00:08:01,580 --> 00:08:03,660
Every ship leaving Baltimore that week did

214
00:08:03,660 --> 00:08:04,560
the same thing.

215
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,460
Every ship leaving most ports

216
00:08:06,460 --> 00:08:08,620
in the world does the same thing tonight.

217
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:10,500
A rule that only looks wrong once,

218
00:08:10,500 --> 00:08:12,820
in hindsight, after a wire fails,

219
00:08:12,820 --> 00:08:14,346
is not the thing that failed.

220
00:08:14,740 --> 00:08:16,160
So walk the four minutes again

221
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:18,371
and ask what lever was actually available.

222
00:08:18,900 --> 00:08:20,840
The emergency generator started and picked

223
00:08:20,840 --> 00:08:22,000
up the emergency bus,

224
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:23,140
exactly as designed.

225
00:08:23,920 --> 00:08:26,440
The engineers found and closed
the breakers by hand.

226
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:27,630
Twice.

227
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:28,560
In the dark.

228
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:30,470
The pilots called for help,

229
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:32,260
warned the water, and told the Coast

230
00:08:32,260 --> 00:08:33,090
Guard.

231
00:08:33,120 --> 00:08:34,541
The shore stopped the cars.

232
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:36,800
And every one of those levers moves

233
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:38,379
something other than the ship.

234
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:42,520
Because the one thing that would have changed
the outcome was the propeller,

235
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:44,320
and the propeller was downstream

236
00:08:44,320 --> 00:08:47,540
of the exact system that kept
switching itself off.

237
00:08:47,980 --> 00:08:49,780
The main engine was a separate machine

238
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:51,844
from the four generators —

239
00:08:51,845 --> 00:08:54,980
but the pumps that cooled it, and the pumps
that drove the steering,

240
00:08:55,380 --> 00:08:58,160
lived on the low-voltage bus that went dark.

241
00:08:58,200 --> 00:08:59,779
That is the shape of the trap.

242
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:02,560
The ship was not disabled
because her engine broke.

243
00:09:03,120 --> 00:09:04,436
Her engine did not break.

244
00:09:04,920 --> 00:09:07,240
She was disabled
because the small electrical

245
00:09:07,240 --> 00:09:09,080
support around a very large engine went

246
00:09:09,080 --> 00:09:11,500
away, and a very large engine without

247
00:09:11,500 --> 00:09:13,580
cooling and without steering assistance

248
00:09:13,580 --> 00:09:15,400
is not a propulsion system.

249
00:09:15,980 --> 00:09:18,460
It is ballast with a crankshaft in it.

250
00:09:18,490 --> 00:09:19,640
There is one more detail

251
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:21,693
in the record that makes this concrete,

252
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:24,909
and it is the sort of thing only
an investigator would notice.

253
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:28,139
The ship's data recorder never
stopped listening.

254
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:30,760
International rules require a vessel

255
00:09:30,760 --> 00:09:33,180
of her tonnage, built after the middle

256
00:09:33,180 --> 00:09:35,920
of 2014 and trading internationally,

257
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:36,820
to carry one —

258
00:09:37,420 --> 00:09:38,660
and through both blackouts,

259
00:09:38,660 --> 00:09:40,081
with most of the ship dark,

260
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:42,407
the bridge audio kept being captured.

261
00:09:42,700 --> 00:09:44,480
Which means we are not reconstructing

262
00:09:44,480 --> 00:09:46,217
this from memory or from lawyers.

263
00:09:46,860 --> 00:09:48,560
There is a recording of four minutes

264
00:09:48,560 --> 00:09:50,300
in which a professional crew did

265
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:53,140
the right things in the right
order and it did not matter.

266
00:09:53,880 --> 00:09:55,520
That is a rare and horrible kind

267
00:09:55,520 --> 00:09:59,180
of evidence, and it forecloses
the comfortable explanation.

268
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:01,420
There is no moment on that tape

269
00:10:01,420 --> 00:10:03,600
where somebody freezes, or argues,

270
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:05,758
or fails to understand what is happening.

271
00:10:06,100 --> 00:10:08,640
What there is, instead, is a ship
that kept coming back

272
00:10:08,640 --> 00:10:09,956
to life from the neck up.

273
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:12,580
At twenty-seven minutes
and thirty-two seconds

274
00:10:12,580 --> 00:10:14,540
past one she had her lights,

275
00:10:14,860 --> 00:10:17,228
her low-voltage bus
and generator number two.

276
00:10:17,940 --> 00:10:22,140
Ninety seconds earlier she had had all
of that too, and then lost it again.

277
00:10:22,540 --> 00:10:23,650
Twice in four minutes,

278
00:10:23,680 --> 00:10:25,260
the thing that was designed never

279
00:10:25,260 --> 00:10:26,576
to happen twice in a row,

280
00:10:26,820 --> 00:10:28,083
happened twice in a row.

281
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:29,900
So the story is not a story

282
00:10:29,900 --> 00:10:31,005
about people failing.

283
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:33,000
It is a story about a machine

284
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:34,500
that was supposed to come back

285
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:37,368
and did not come back in time —

286
00:10:37,369 --> 00:10:41,080
and about the difference between getting
your lights back and getting your ship back.

287
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:45,460
At twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two seconds
past one, the Dali had electricity.

288
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:47,120
She had lighting.

289
00:10:47,760 --> 00:10:49,300
She had her low-voltage bus,

290
00:10:49,300 --> 00:10:51,080
fed from generator number two.

291
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:52,860
What she did not have

292
00:10:52,860 --> 00:10:54,123
was a turning propeller,

293
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:56,400
and without a turning propeller a rudder

294
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:57,663
is a piece of furniture.

295
00:10:57,980 --> 00:10:59,080
Water has to move across

296
00:10:59,080 --> 00:11:01,280
it for it to do anything at all.

297
00:11:01,400 --> 00:11:02,920
She was, at that moment,

298
00:11:03,260 --> 00:11:06,480
nine hundred and forty-seven feet of loaded

299
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:08,920
steel with the lights on,

300
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:12,200
going where the tide and her own momentum
took her. Which leaves one question,

301
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:14,140
and it is the question two federal

302
00:11:14,140 --> 00:11:15,929
investigations spent two years on.

303
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:18,197
Why did the power keep going out?

304
00:11:20,140 --> 00:11:22,020
Before the failures, the reasons.

305
00:11:22,780 --> 00:11:24,480
This is a system that was designed

306
00:11:24,480 --> 00:11:26,800
by people who had thought
about this exact night.

307
00:11:27,480 --> 00:11:29,760
A vessel like the Dali distributes power

308
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:32,447
on a high-voltage bus fed
by four diesel generators.

309
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:35,260
A four-hundred-and-forty-volt
low-voltage bus

310
00:11:35,260 --> 00:11:38,000
hangs off it through two
step-down transformers.

311
00:11:38,580 --> 00:11:40,760
The low-voltage side is where the ordinary

312
00:11:40,760 --> 00:11:41,971
life of the ship lives:

313
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:44,560
the lighting, the steering gear pumps,

314
00:11:45,300 --> 00:11:47,195
the main engine cooling water pumps.

315
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:48,900
The redundancy is deliberate

316
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:49,960
and it is doubled.

317
00:11:50,700 --> 00:11:52,600
Two transformers, so one can fail.

318
00:11:53,460 --> 00:11:56,161
Breakers either side of each,
so a fault can be isolated.

319
00:11:56,940 --> 00:12:00,080
Generators that restart on their own,
so a blackout does not need

320
00:12:00,080 --> 00:12:01,501
a human in the loop to end.

321
00:12:01,980 --> 00:12:03,280
That is the design.

322
00:12:04,020 --> 00:12:04,920
Now the night.

323
00:12:05,420 --> 00:12:07,157
Ten hours before the Dali sailed,

324
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:10,260
while she was still tied up in Baltimore,
the lights went out

325
00:12:10,260 --> 00:12:11,313
on her once already.

326
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:15,170
A crewmember was working on the exhaust
scrubber system for the engine driving

327
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:16,980
the only generator that was online.

328
00:12:17,680 --> 00:12:20,240
He closed an inline exhaust
damper by mistake.

329
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:22,179
The exhaust had nowhere to go.

330
00:12:22,620 --> 00:12:24,480
The generator stopped, and the ship went

331
00:12:24,480 --> 00:12:25,380
dark at the pier.

332
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:30,345
Then it happened a second time
in port, for a different reason —

333
00:12:30,346 --> 00:12:32,740
not enough fuel pressure
for the online generator.

334
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:34,692
Nobody died in either of those.

335
00:12:35,180 --> 00:12:39,140
They are the kind of thing that gets written
in a log and talked about over a meal.

336
00:12:39,960 --> 00:12:42,900
So why would a night at a pier
where nothing happened turn

337
00:12:42,900 --> 00:12:45,380
out to matter more than anything
else in this story?

338
00:12:45,760 --> 00:12:47,340
Because of what was done to put

339
00:12:47,340 --> 00:12:48,708
the ship right afterwards.

340
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:50,880
Something happened during the recovery

341
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:52,617
from the second in-port blackout,

342
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:54,060
and it is the hinge

343
00:12:54,060 --> 00:12:55,113
of the entire night.

344
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:57,220
Coming back up, the crew changed

345
00:12:57,220 --> 00:12:59,115
the ship's electrical configuration.

346
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:01,980
They stopped using breakers HR2 and LR2

347
00:13:01,980 --> 00:13:03,618
with transformer TR2 —

348
00:13:03,980 --> 00:13:06,993
which had been in use for several months —

349
00:13:06,994 --> 00:13:09,140
and switched to HR1, LR1 and TR1.

350
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:11,660
And that is the configuration the Dali

351
00:13:11,660 --> 00:13:13,081
sailed on the next morning.

352
00:13:13,480 --> 00:13:15,100
Ten hours later, six tenths

353
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:16,550
of a mile from the Key Bridge,

354
00:13:16,580 --> 00:13:18,800
it was HR1 and LR1 that opened.

355
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:22,872
It took the National Transportation Safety
Board twenty months to say why.

356
00:13:23,460 --> 00:13:24,830
On the eighteenth of November,

357
00:13:24,860 --> 00:13:27,180
2025, the Board met and determined

358
00:13:27,180 --> 00:13:28,180
the probable cause.

359
00:13:28,800 --> 00:13:29,780
It was a wire.

360
00:13:30,680 --> 00:13:32,740
Specifically: a loose signal wire connection

361
00:13:32,740 --> 00:13:33,860
to a terminal block,

362
00:13:33,860 --> 00:13:36,380
stemming from the improper installation

363
00:13:36,380 --> 00:13:39,000
of wire-label banding, which caused the loss

364
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,400
of electrical power, which caused the loss

365
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:43,320
of propulsion and steering close

366
00:13:43,320 --> 00:13:44,220
to the bridge.

367
00:13:44,700 --> 00:13:47,393
The mechanism is small enough
to hold between two fingers.

368
00:13:47,980 --> 00:13:50,160
A terminal block has a spring-clamp gate

369
00:13:50,160 --> 00:13:51,107
that grips a wire.

370
00:13:51,680 --> 00:13:53,940
Somebody had put a band of labelling
around that wire.

371
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:57,260
The band stopped the wire seating
fully into the gate.

372
00:13:57,640 --> 00:13:59,219
The connection was inadequate.

373
00:13:59,580 --> 00:14:00,843
That is the whole thing.

374
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:02,440
A ship of this size,

375
00:14:02,830 --> 00:14:04,380
a bridge of this size,

376
00:14:05,120 --> 00:14:06,980
six men, and the physical cause

377
00:14:06,980 --> 00:14:08,560
is a label that was a fraction

378
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:10,020
of a millimetre too thick

379
00:14:10,020 --> 00:14:11,020
in the wrong place.

380
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:13,567
But that explains the first blackout.

381
00:14:13,780 --> 00:14:15,380
It does not explain why she did

382
00:14:15,380 --> 00:14:16,280
not come back.

383
00:14:16,900 --> 00:14:19,001
Because remember: she
was built to come back.

384
00:14:19,960 --> 00:14:20,907
Automatic restart.

385
00:14:21,200 --> 00:14:22,358
Reliable redundancies.

386
00:14:22,980 --> 00:14:24,927
That was the promise in the drawings.

387
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:26,450
On the twelfth of May,

388
00:14:26,480 --> 00:14:29,920
2026, a federal court in Maryland unsealed

389
00:14:29,920 --> 00:14:32,620
an indictment, and the United States offered

390
00:14:32,620 --> 00:14:34,220
its account of why the promise

391
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:35,120
was not kept.

392
00:14:35,780 --> 00:14:36,860
Before a word of it:

393
00:14:37,100 --> 00:14:39,090
an indictment is merely an accusation,

394
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:41,840
and all defendants are presumed
innocent until

395
00:14:41,840 --> 00:14:43,980
proven guilty beyond a reasonable doubt

396
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:44,927
in a court of law.

397
00:14:45,300 --> 00:14:47,620
Those are the Department of Justice's own

398
00:14:47,620 --> 00:14:50,146
words, printed underneath
its own press release.

399
00:14:50,620 --> 00:14:52,360
This programme will hold to them.

400
00:14:52,980 --> 00:14:56,020
The three defendants are Synergy
Marine, of Singapore;

401
00:14:56,900 --> 00:14:58,900
Synergy Maritime, of Chennai,

402
00:14:59,140 --> 00:14:59,830
India;

403
00:14:59,860 --> 00:15:01,760
and Radhakrishnan Karthik Nair,

404
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:04,880
forty-seven years old, an Indian national

405
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:08,780
who worked for both companies as the technical
superintendent for the Dali.

406
00:15:09,540 --> 00:15:11,540
They are charged with conspiracy to defraud

407
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:14,080
the United States, with willfully failing

408
00:15:14,080 --> 00:15:15,840
to immediately inform the Coast Guard

409
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:17,472
of a known hazardous condition,

410
00:15:17,940 --> 00:15:20,098
with obstruction of an agency proceeding,

411
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:21,600
and with false statements.

412
00:15:22,320 --> 00:15:24,760
The two companies additionally
face misdemeanour

413
00:15:24,760 --> 00:15:26,680
environmental counts for what went

414
00:15:26,680 --> 00:15:29,081
into the Patapsco River —

415
00:15:29,082 --> 00:15:31,134
containers, oil, and the bridge itself.

416
00:15:31,900 --> 00:15:34,163
The allegation about the machinery is this.

417
00:15:34,500 --> 00:15:37,680
The government says the Dali lost power
twice in a four-minute span,

418
00:15:38,080 --> 00:15:39,340
and that a loose wire

419
00:15:39,340 --> 00:15:41,880
in a high-voltage switchboard likely caused

420
00:15:41,880 --> 00:15:42,780
the first loss.

421
00:15:43,420 --> 00:15:46,840
And then, the government says,
the ship did not recover the way

422
00:15:46,840 --> 00:15:49,129
she was designed to recover —

423
00:15:49,130 --> 00:15:51,130
because the defendants had altered her.

424
00:15:51,160 --> 00:15:52,634
According to the indictment,

425
00:15:52,840 --> 00:15:54,300
they relied on a flushing pump

426
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:55,620
to supply fuel to two

427
00:15:55,620 --> 00:15:57,199
of the Dali's four generators.

428
00:15:57,520 --> 00:15:59,740
A flushing pump is a housekeeping device.

429
00:16:00,460 --> 00:16:02,020
It is for moving fuel around,

430
00:16:02,280 --> 00:16:04,122
not for feeding an engine in anger.

431
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:05,880
And the crucial property,

432
00:16:05,880 --> 00:16:07,354
in the government's telling,

433
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:09,160
is that it was not designed

434
00:16:09,160 --> 00:16:11,371
to automatically restart after a blackout.

435
00:16:12,100 --> 00:16:14,140
So the chain the indictment draws is:

436
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:15,587
the lights go out,

437
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:17,256
the generators need fuel,

438
00:16:17,700 --> 00:16:19,420
the pump that was feeding them cannot

439
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:21,420
start itself, and the ship blacks out

440
00:16:21,420 --> 00:16:22,320
a second time.

441
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:24,400
And then comes the sentence that will

442
00:16:24,400 --> 00:16:26,189
be fought over in court for years.

443
00:16:26,960 --> 00:16:30,900
The indictment alleges that if the Dali
had used the proper fuel supply pumps,

444
00:16:31,560 --> 00:16:35,580
the vessel would have regained power in time
to navigate safely under the Key Bridge.

445
00:16:35,960 --> 00:16:36,860
Alleges.

446
00:16:37,140 --> 00:16:38,040
Not found.

447
00:16:38,620 --> 00:16:39,520
Not proven.

448
00:16:40,080 --> 00:16:41,420
A grand jury heard a case

449
00:16:41,420 --> 00:16:42,578
and returned a charge,

450
00:16:43,120 --> 00:16:44,383
and that is a beginning,

451
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:45,600
not an ending.

452
00:16:45,960 --> 00:16:47,381
And then, five weeks later,

453
00:16:47,440 --> 00:16:50,460
something happened
that is not an allegation at all.

454
00:16:50,860 --> 00:16:52,070
On the eighteenth of June,

455
00:16:52,100 --> 00:16:54,800
2026, the chief engineer of the Dali

456
00:16:54,800 --> 00:16:56,960
entered into a deferred
prosecution agreement

457
00:16:56,960 --> 00:16:58,260
with the United States.

458
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:00,947
His name is Karthikeyan Deenadayalan.

459
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:02,896
He was aboard that night.

460
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:06,542
In that agreement he admitted to failing
to report a hazardous condition,

461
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:08,920
in violation of the Ports and Waterways

462
00:17:08,920 --> 00:17:09,820
Safety Act.

463
00:17:10,140 --> 00:17:14,440
And the things he acknowledged are worth hearing
slowly. He had also been chief engineer

464
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:17,741
on two of the Dali's sister ships —

465
00:17:17,742 --> 00:17:19,584
the Maersk Saltoro and the Cezanne.

466
00:17:19,800 --> 00:17:23,680
And he knew that all three of them
used an unsafe fuel supply pump.

467
00:17:23,860 --> 00:17:24,760
Not one ship.

468
00:17:25,340 --> 00:17:26,240
Three.

469
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:28,590
He acknowledged that the pump
lacked redundancy,

470
00:17:28,620 --> 00:17:30,700
and that this compromised the vessels' safe

471
00:17:30,700 --> 00:17:33,000
navigation and their ability to recover

472
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:34,105
from a loss of power.

473
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:36,040
And he knew that a ship unable

474
00:17:36,040 --> 00:17:37,860
to recover from a loss of power

475
00:17:37,860 --> 00:17:39,540
could adversely affect the safety

476
00:17:39,540 --> 00:17:41,967
of the vessel itself —

477
00:17:41,968 --> 00:17:45,880
and of any bridge, structure or shore area.
That sentence was written in a legal

478
00:17:45,880 --> 00:17:47,260
agreement in 2026.

479
00:17:47,960 --> 00:17:49,700
It describes, in the abstract,

480
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:52,560
exactly what happened
in the Patapsco in 2024.

481
00:17:53,380 --> 00:17:54,640
He also said he had spoken

482
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:57,080
and corresponded with people
at Synergy about

483
00:17:57,080 --> 00:17:59,396
using that pump, including the co-defendant,

484
00:17:59,820 --> 00:18:00,670
Nair.

485
00:18:00,700 --> 00:18:02,121
And he said something else,

486
00:18:02,260 --> 00:18:03,920
and here the guardrail goes back up,

487
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:07,640
because this is one man's account of another
man's conduct and that other

488
00:18:07,640 --> 00:18:08,851
man has not been tried.

489
00:18:09,460 --> 00:18:11,500
The chief engineer said that Nair directed

490
00:18:11,500 --> 00:18:13,120
him to send a convincing email

491
00:18:13,120 --> 00:18:14,480
to the ship's charterer,

492
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:16,280
so that the charterer would not ask

493
00:18:16,280 --> 00:18:18,820
more questions about fuel
consumption and find

494
00:18:18,820 --> 00:18:20,860
out that the flushing pump was being used.

495
00:18:21,540 --> 00:18:22,803
There is one more count,

496
00:18:22,940 --> 00:18:27,570
and it is the one that tells you what kind
of story this is. The obstruction charge relates,

497
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:29,600
among other things, to what Nair

498
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:31,811
is alleged to have told the investigators:

499
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:33,500
that he was unaware the Dali

500
00:18:33,500 --> 00:18:36,060
was using the flushing pump
to fuel the generators.

501
00:18:36,380 --> 00:18:37,720
An alteration to a ship.

502
00:18:38,380 --> 00:18:39,300
In an engine room.

503
00:18:39,300 --> 00:18:40,800
Below the waterline.

504
00:18:41,240 --> 00:18:42,740
Which, the United States says,

505
00:18:42,740 --> 00:18:44,500
the investigators had to find rather

506
00:18:44,500 --> 00:18:45,400
than be told.

507
00:18:45,820 --> 00:18:47,970
And what about the bridge in all this?

508
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,526
The Board looked at that too,

509
00:18:49,720 --> 00:18:51,400
and its finding is quieter and,

510
00:18:51,400 --> 00:18:53,680
for anyone who drives, worse.

511
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:56,367
Contributing to the collapse
and to the loss of life

512
00:18:56,260 --> 00:18:58,400
was the absence of countermeasures to reduce

513
00:18:58,400 --> 00:19:00,660
the bridge's vulnerability to being struck

514
00:19:00,660 --> 00:19:03,033
by an ocean-going vessel —

515
00:19:03,034 --> 00:19:06,820
countermeasures that could have been put in place
if the Maryland Transportation Authority

516
00:19:06,820 --> 00:19:09,083
had carried out a vulnerability assessment.

517
00:19:09,220 --> 00:19:12,120
The guidance recommending
those assessments is longstanding.

518
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:17,021
It comes from the American Association of State
Highway and Transportation Officials,

519
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:19,360
and it has been sitting there for years.

520
00:19:19,820 --> 00:19:21,640
And here the Board did something careful

521
00:19:21,640 --> 00:19:22,940
that is worth noticing.

522
00:19:23,660 --> 00:19:26,040
It did not find that Maryland
knew and ignored it.

523
00:19:26,280 --> 00:19:28,160
It found that they were likely unaware

524
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:29,318
of the potential risk.

525
00:19:29,920 --> 00:19:30,820
Not a cover-up.

526
00:19:31,440 --> 00:19:33,820
Something more ordinary and harder to fix:

527
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:36,060
a recommendation that existed,

528
00:19:36,060 --> 00:19:37,680
and a bridge whose owner did

529
00:19:37,680 --> 00:19:39,154
not know it applied to them.

530
00:19:39,460 --> 00:19:42,580
A vulnerability assessment sounds
like a filing cabinet.

531
00:19:42,680 --> 00:19:43,580
It is a sum.

532
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:45,320
Take the bridge you have.

533
00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:49,320
Take the ships that use the channel
now, not the ships that used

534
00:19:49,320 --> 00:19:50,846
it when the bridge was drawn.

535
00:19:51,200 --> 00:19:52,840
Work out what happens to that pier

536
00:19:52,840 --> 00:19:54,520
if that ship arrives at that speed

537
00:19:54,520 --> 00:19:55,420
at that angle.

538
00:19:55,760 --> 00:20:00,477
Then find out whether the protection you already
have is protection. Or decoration.

539
00:20:00,780 --> 00:20:02,938
The dolphins by pier seventeen were real.

540
00:20:03,460 --> 00:20:04,720
Sheet pile and concrete,

541
00:20:04,720 --> 00:20:06,720
built by people who were not careless.

542
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:09,220
But protection is never absolute.

543
00:20:09,940 --> 00:20:11,680
It is protection against something,

544
00:20:11,680 --> 00:20:13,800
at some speed, of some size.

545
00:20:14,600 --> 00:20:15,880
And a bridge stays exactly

546
00:20:15,880 --> 00:20:17,820
where it is while the ships underneath

547
00:20:17,820 --> 00:20:19,031
it keep getting bigger.

548
00:20:19,440 --> 00:20:20,580
That is the failure.

549
00:20:21,260 --> 00:20:22,160
Not neglect.

550
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:24,858
Arithmetic that went quietly out of date.

551
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:27,380
So how many other bridges are standing

552
00:20:27,380 --> 00:20:30,100
over shipping channels tonight
without anyone

553
00:20:30,100 --> 00:20:31,205
having done that sum?

554
00:20:32,940 --> 00:20:34,540
The Board answered that question

555
00:20:34,540 --> 00:20:36,520
on the twentieth of March, 2025.

556
00:20:38,120 --> 00:20:40,560
Sixty-eight. Sixty-eight bridges
in the United States

557
00:20:40,560 --> 00:20:43,940
for which the NTSB recommended
a vulnerability evaluation.

558
00:20:44,560 --> 00:20:46,740
Not sixty-eight bridges that are unsafe.

559
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:50,419
Sixty-eight bridges where nobody
had done the arithmetic —

560
00:20:50,760 --> 00:20:52,600
where the owner could not say what

561
00:20:52,600 --> 00:20:54,300
would happen if a modern ship hit

562
00:20:54,300 --> 00:20:56,620
a pier, because the question had never

563
00:20:56,620 --> 00:20:57,883
been asked in that form.

564
00:20:58,360 --> 00:21:00,518
Every one of those is somebody's commute.

565
00:21:00,900 --> 00:21:04,420
Meanwhile, the money moved through a different
courthouse, and it moved fast.

566
00:21:05,120 --> 00:21:06,500
Six days after the collapse,

567
00:21:06,500 --> 00:21:07,658
on the first of April,

568
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:12,116
2024, the ship's owner and manager —

569
00:21:12,117 --> 00:21:16,133
Grace Ocean Private Limited and Synergy
Marine Private Limited —

570
00:21:16,134 --> 00:21:20,840
filed in the District of Maryland for exoneration
from, or limitation of, liability.

571
00:21:21,460 --> 00:21:22,820
That is a very old piece

572
00:21:22,820 --> 00:21:24,180
of American maritime law,

573
00:21:24,700 --> 00:21:26,840
and it does something that sounds impossible

574
00:21:26,840 --> 00:21:28,051
when you first hear it.

575
00:21:28,200 --> 00:21:29,840
It allows the owner of a vessel

576
00:21:29,840 --> 00:21:31,360
to ask a court to cap what

577
00:21:31,360 --> 00:21:34,260
they can be made to pay,
at roughly the value of what

578
00:21:34,260 --> 00:21:35,313
is left of the ship.

579
00:21:35,880 --> 00:21:38,640
Before the outrage, the reason.

580
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:41,960
That law exists because of a hard problem.

581
00:21:42,240 --> 00:21:43,872
A ship is an asset that leaves.

582
00:21:44,620 --> 00:21:45,720
She sails out of reach,

583
00:21:45,720 --> 00:21:47,720
under people her owner cannot see,

584
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:49,820
into weather nobody controls.

585
00:21:50,680 --> 00:21:52,540
If one voyage could destroy the company

586
00:21:52,540 --> 00:21:54,940
that financed it, the argument runs,

587
00:21:55,380 --> 00:21:56,720
nobody finances ships.

588
00:21:57,440 --> 00:21:58,860
So the law strikes a deal.

589
00:21:59,680 --> 00:22:02,620
An owner who did not know about the fault,
and could not have known,

590
00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:04,380
may ask a court to cap what

591
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:06,779
he owes at the value of the vessel
and her freight.

592
00:22:07,060 --> 00:22:08,720
In the abstract it is a sensible

593
00:22:08,720 --> 00:22:10,088
rule for a trading nation.

594
00:22:10,720 --> 00:22:12,160
After a bridge falls on a work

595
00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:13,380
crew it is a number.

596
00:22:14,100 --> 00:22:18,777
The figure they asked to be limited
to was about forty-three point seven million

597
00:22:18,799 --> 00:22:21,580
dollars. Against that, the United States put

598
00:22:21,580 --> 00:22:23,720
in a claim of one hundred

599
00:22:24,340 --> 00:22:27,760
and three million, seventy-eight thousand

600
00:22:27,760 --> 00:22:30,040
and fifty-six dollars, under the Rivers

601
00:22:30,880 --> 00:22:33,320
and Harbors Act, the Oil Pollution Act,

602
00:22:33,320 --> 00:22:36,903
and general maritime law — for what it had cost
the government to answer the collapse.

603
00:22:36,440 --> 00:22:39,000
The companies agreed to pay one hundred

604
00:22:39,660 --> 00:22:43,440
and one million, nine hundred and eighty thousand
dollars. That is a settlement of a civil

605
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:44,340
claim.

606
00:22:44,460 --> 00:22:46,302
It is not an admission of anything,

607
00:22:46,620 --> 00:22:48,357
and it is not a criminal finding,

608
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:51,581
and this programme is not going
to let it stand in for one.

609
00:22:52,200 --> 00:22:53,720
Then, in May of 2026,

610
00:22:53,820 --> 00:22:55,188
the criminal case arrived,

611
00:22:55,360 --> 00:22:56,980
and with it a number that resets

612
00:22:56,980 --> 00:22:58,559
the scale of everything above.

613
00:22:59,080 --> 00:23:01,100
The indictment alleges the economic loss

614
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:03,592
in this case is at least
five billion dollars.

615
00:23:03,593 --> 00:23:05,660
Five billion, against a request

616
00:23:05,660 --> 00:23:07,900
to be capped at forty-three point seven

617
00:23:08,159 --> 00:23:10,340
million. The gap between those two numbers

618
00:23:10,340 --> 00:23:11,620
is the entire argument,

619
00:23:12,080 --> 00:23:13,700
and it will be argued by real

620
00:23:13,700 --> 00:23:15,805
lawyers in a real courtroom in Maryland,

621
00:23:16,220 --> 00:23:17,820
and it is not finished today.

622
00:23:18,500 --> 00:23:22,560
The investigation that produced the charges
ran for two years and involved

623
00:23:22,560 --> 00:23:25,400
the FBI, the Coast Guard
Investigative Service,

624
00:23:25,940 --> 00:23:28,340
and the criminal investigators
of the Environmental

625
00:23:28,340 --> 00:23:30,920
Protection Agency, some of whom were among

626
00:23:30,920 --> 00:23:33,078
the first people aboard the wrecked ship.

627
00:23:33,440 --> 00:23:34,760
It is worth saying plainly

628
00:23:34,760 --> 00:23:36,640
where that leaves things on the day

629
00:23:36,640 --> 00:23:37,851
this programme is made.

630
00:23:38,280 --> 00:23:39,859
The Safety Board has finished.

631
00:23:40,180 --> 00:23:41,420
It met, it voted,

632
00:23:41,540 --> 00:23:42,908
it named a probable cause,

633
00:23:43,160 --> 00:23:45,800
and it said the complete
report would follow.

634
00:23:45,980 --> 00:23:47,760
The civil claim by the United States

635
00:23:47,760 --> 00:23:49,360
is resolved, by agreement,

636
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:51,798
for a hundred and one million,

637
00:23:51,829 --> 00:23:53,784
nine hundred and eighty thousand dollars.

638
00:23:53,785 --> 00:23:55,838
And the criminal case has barely begun.

639
00:23:56,600 --> 00:23:58,480
An indictment was unsealed in May.

640
00:23:59,280 --> 00:24:01,145
Three defendants stand charged —

641
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:02,648
two companies and one man.

642
00:24:03,300 --> 00:24:04,774
None of them has been tried.

643
00:24:05,180 --> 00:24:07,300
None of them has been convicted of anything.

644
00:24:07,720 --> 00:24:10,840
The presumption of innocence
is not a formality that a documentary

645
00:24:10,840 --> 00:24:13,760
gets to skip because the facts
underneath are upsetting.

646
00:24:14,240 --> 00:24:17,120
There is also a distinction
in that indictment which is easy

647
00:24:17,120 --> 00:24:18,700
to miss and which will decide

648
00:24:18,700 --> 00:24:19,600
a great deal.

649
00:24:19,980 --> 00:24:21,000
The counts are not,

650
00:24:21,000 --> 00:24:23,500
in the main, about building a bad ship.

651
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:27,802
They are about what was done with a good one,
and about what was said afterwards.

652
00:24:28,740 --> 00:24:29,640
Conspiracy.

653
00:24:30,320 --> 00:24:33,280
Failing to tell the Coast Guard
about a known hazardous condition.

654
00:24:34,100 --> 00:24:35,995
Obstruction of an agency proceeding.

655
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:37,460
False statements.

656
00:24:38,100 --> 00:24:40,460
Read them in order and they
describe a sequence:

657
00:24:40,940 --> 00:24:42,800
something was changed, the change

658
00:24:42,800 --> 00:24:45,140
was not reported, and when investigators
came

659
00:24:45,140 --> 00:24:46,660
asking, the government says

660
00:24:46,660 --> 00:24:47,660
they were not told.

661
00:24:48,220 --> 00:24:49,820
Whether that sequence is true

662
00:24:49,820 --> 00:24:51,720
is not for this programme to decide,

663
00:24:51,720 --> 00:24:53,800
and it is not decided yet.

664
00:24:54,500 --> 00:24:57,300
It is for twelve people in a courtroom
in Maryland who have

665
00:24:57,300 --> 00:24:58,353
not yet been chosen.

666
00:24:59,020 --> 00:25:00,700
And all of it —

667
00:25:00,701 --> 00:25:03,061
the Board's twenty months,
the grand jury's two years,

668
00:25:03,440 --> 00:25:05,598
the settlement, the sixty-eight bridges —

669
00:25:06,300 --> 00:25:07,360
all of it comes back

670
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:09,996
to a stretch of time you
could hold your breath through.

671
00:25:10,200 --> 00:25:11,780
The duty officer's order went out

672
00:25:11,780 --> 00:25:14,780
at twenty-seven minutes and fifty-three
seconds past one.

673
00:25:15,140 --> 00:25:16,780
The ship touched the pier at about

674
00:25:16,780 --> 00:25:18,306
twenty-nine minutes past one.

675
00:25:18,860 --> 00:25:19,913
Sixty-seven seconds.

676
00:25:20,480 --> 00:25:22,120
And in that window a decision made

677
00:25:22,120 --> 00:25:23,780
ten hours earlier at a pier,

678
00:25:23,780 --> 00:25:26,280
and a modification made at some point

679
00:25:26,280 --> 00:25:27,859
before that in an engine room,

680
00:25:28,280 --> 00:25:29,240
and a band of label

681
00:25:29,240 --> 00:25:30,240
on a single wire,

682
00:25:30,240 --> 00:25:32,020
all arrived at the same place

683
00:25:32,020 --> 00:25:32,920
at the same time.

684
00:25:33,460 --> 00:25:34,540
So what is the rule

685
00:25:34,540 --> 00:25:36,520
that was actually running that night?

686
00:25:38,380 --> 00:25:39,500
Go back to the deck.

687
00:25:40,180 --> 00:25:41,864
The work light is still burning.

688
00:25:42,260 --> 00:25:44,140
The cone is still standing in it.

689
00:25:44,700 --> 00:25:46,016
Nothing has happened yet.

690
00:25:46,460 --> 00:25:48,280
Down the channel, a ship is leaving

691
00:25:48,280 --> 00:25:49,701
a berth with her lights on.

692
00:25:50,100 --> 00:25:51,626
Every lamp aboard her is lit.

693
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:54,780
That is what a working ship looks like.

694
00:25:55,060 --> 00:25:57,113
She loses them at twenty-five past one.

695
00:25:57,480 --> 00:25:58,480
She gets them back.

696
00:25:58,960 --> 00:26:00,065
She loses them again.

697
00:26:00,820 --> 00:26:02,800
And at twenty-seven minutes and thirty-two

698
00:26:02,800 --> 00:26:05,600
seconds past one, with two tenths

699
00:26:05,600 --> 00:26:06,500
of a mile to run,

700
00:26:06,740 --> 00:26:08,840
she gets them back for the last time.

701
00:26:09,100 --> 00:26:10,258
Lit from bow to stern.

702
00:26:10,760 --> 00:26:11,760
Every lamp burning.

703
00:26:12,500 --> 00:26:14,342
And not moving under her own power.

704
00:26:15,080 --> 00:26:17,185
The drawings promised she could recover.

705
00:26:17,420 --> 00:26:18,788
They were honest drawings.

706
00:26:19,340 --> 00:26:21,866
Somewhere between those drawings
and that water,

707
00:26:22,260 --> 00:26:23,340
the United States says,

708
00:26:23,340 --> 00:26:24,912
the promise was edited —

709
00:26:25,640 --> 00:26:27,850
and the edit stayed in the engine room.

710
00:26:27,880 --> 00:26:29,512
Nobody has been tried for that.

711
00:26:29,900 --> 00:26:32,900
On the other side of the channel,
the guidance about ship strikes had

712
00:26:32,900 --> 00:26:34,480
been written, and published,

713
00:26:34,480 --> 00:26:36,000
and had not reached the people

714
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:37,105
who owned the bridge.

715
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:39,438
The Board found they were likely unaware.

716
00:26:40,120 --> 00:26:41,020
Not a villain.

717
00:26:41,420 --> 00:26:42,320
A gap.

718
00:26:42,940 --> 00:26:45,840
Sixty-eight bridges are now
on a list that says:

719
00:26:46,020 --> 00:26:47,180
go and check what happens

720
00:26:47,180 --> 00:26:48,460
if a ship hits this.

721
00:26:49,000 --> 00:26:50,440
That list did not exist

722
00:26:50,440 --> 00:26:52,640
on the twenty-fifth of March, 2024.

723
00:26:53,520 --> 00:26:55,700
The work light on the deck goes off at dawn.

724
00:26:56,140 --> 00:26:57,982
The cone gets stacked on the truck.

725
00:26:58,480 --> 00:26:59,380
The lane opens.

726
00:26:59,920 --> 00:27:02,360
Every light in this story came back on.

727
00:27:02,420 --> 00:27:04,840
That was never the thing that had to hold.
